=== The figurate DEGREE ladder (why Pollock != pentagonal) ===

DEGREE 1 (the LINE SEGMENT, 2-gon): P_2(m) = m = the integers themselves.
   Waring bound for 2-gonal = 1 (trivial: every n IS a 2-gonal number).

DEGREE 2 (POLYGONS, quadratic in m -> Cauchy/Gauss/Lagrange, EXACT bound = k):
   3-gonal triangular(Eureka,Gauss)  : Waring bound = 3  (Cauchy: = k? True)
   4-gonal square(Lagrange)          : Waring bound = 4  (Cauchy: = k? True)
   5-gonal pentagonal(Cauchy)        : Waring bound = 5  (Cauchy: = k? True)
   6-gonal hexagonal                 : Waring bound = 6  (Cauchy: = k? True)
   7-gonal heptagonal                : Waring bound = 7  (Cauchy: = k? True)
   8-gonal octagonal                 : Waring bound = 8  (Cauchy: = k? True)
   => degree-2 figurate: bound is the CLEAN formula k, proved by QUADRATIC FORMS (sums of 3 squares).

DEGREE 3 (POLYTOPES, cubic in m -> Pollock, circle-method, NO clean formula):
   tetrahedral C(m+2,3)    : Waring bound = 5
   octahedral m(2m^2+1)/3  : Waring bound = 7
   cube m^3                : Waring bound = 9
   => degree-3 figurate: bounds are MESSY (tetrahedral conj 5 but only 8 proven; cube=9=Waring;
      icosahedral was conjectured 13 but is really 15). Proved (where proved) by the CIRCLE METHOD
      (Linnik ternary-quadratic-form reduction), NOT Cauchy's elementary quadratic-form lemma.

THE ANSWER: Pollock (degree 3, cubic) differs from pentagonal (degree 2, quadratic) by exactly the
WARING DEGREE of the figurate polynomial. Degree 2 = sums-of-squares world (exact, clean bound k,
Gauss/Lagrange/Cauchy). Degree 3 = Waring-for-cubes world (asymptotic + finite check, messy bounds,
circle method). The figurate DIMENSION (1 segment, 2 polygon, 3 polytope) = the polynomial degree =
the difficulty class. (Mirrors the OCF spectral hierarchy: c3,c5 = low-degree power sums (spectral,
clean), H = higher (non-spectral, conflict-graph) -- THM-498/499/501.)
